{{!-- ONE ENCOUNTER, wherever it is rendered. Two lists print this: the live one at the top of the
      tab and the Completed fold under it, and the card is the SAME card in both — everything a GM
      gathered stays gathered, stays openable, stays deployable and stays droppable-into after it
      has been run. What changes between the two is one button at the foot of the body, and it
      changes off `used`, which is the very flag the context split the lists by
      (gm-encounters-tab.js): the card can
      therefore never disagree with the list it was printed into, and no caller has to pass a
      "which list am I in" parameter down. That matters more than it looks — Handlebars MERGES a
      partial's hash into the caller's context, so a parameter here would ALSO be a bare lookup
      against the encounter's own fields, and this partial is rendered inside an `{{#each}}` over
      a view-model built in JS. section-heading.hbs states the rule at length.

      THE DRAG HANDLE IS THE NAME BUTTON, NOT THE ROW. In Chromium a `draggable` ancestor takes
      the mousedown from every input inside it, so a draggable <li> would leave the per-entry note
      box unselectable — you could not put a caret in it. Narrowing the handle to the
      icon-and-name control puts the note outside the draggable subtree. The button carries
      `data-uuid` and `data-doc-type` because the payload it emits is CORE'S OWN `{type, uuid}`:
      the same thing the sidebar puts on the wire, so dragging a monster out of here onto the
      canvas behaves exactly like dragging it out of the sidebar. An unresolved row is
      `draggable="false"` — there is nothing on the other end of it to drag.

      THERE IS NO <img> ANYWHERE ON THIS CARD, which is why nothing here carries the
      `draggable="false"` opt-out that every portrait in the system does (see
      tests/actors/steading/steading-actor-drag.test.js). Add a portrait and it needs one, or
      Gecko hands over the image instead of the document.

      NO FIELD HERE CARRIES A `name`. Every one of them is written by hand to
      `actor.system.encounters`; a `name` on an input inside this sheet's <form> puts it in core's
      submit data, where the array indices would expand into an OBJECT keyed "0", "1", ... and
      quietly replace the list with it.

      EXPANDING IS A CLASS, NOT A RENDER. The body is emitted for every encounter and hidden by
      `.is-collapsed`, so the caret toggles it in place: instant, and incapable of landing a
      re-render on a note box two rows down that happens to have focus. --}}
<li class="stonetop-gm-encounter{{#if used}} is-used{{/if}}{{#unless open}} is-collapsed{{/unless}}"
    data-encounter-id="{{id}}">
	<div class="stonetop-gm-encounter-head">
		<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-grip" draggable="true" aria-hidden="true"
		      data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.reorder"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
			<i class="fas fa-grip-vertical"></i>
		</span>
		{{!-- The name IS the input, not a label with a pencil beside it: an encounter gets
		      renamed as often as it gets used ("the bridge" becomes "the bridge at dusk"
		      becomes "the ambush"), and a dialog for a one-line edit is a dialog nobody
		      opens. alt+Up / alt+Down in this box reorders the encounter WITHIN ITS OWN
		      LIST, which is the keyboard half of the grip to its left. --}}
		<input type="text" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-name" value="{{name}}" maxlength="120"
		       aria-label="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.nameLabel"}}">
		{{!-- The head of the note, riding on the row while it is shut (the CSS shows it only under
		      .is-collapsed, where the body it belongs to is hidden). Two bundles both called "the
		      bridge" are told apart by what was written about them, and a column of eight shut
		      cards otherwise says nothing at all. Plain text rather than the enriched HTML below:
		      this is one clipped line, and a content link or a paragraph in it would fight the
		      ellipsis. --}}
		{{#if notesPeek}}<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-peek">{{notesPeek}}</span>{{/if}}
		{{!-- WHAT IS IN IT, BY KIND, and the one thing on the shut row that is about the bundle
		      rather than about the GM's own words. "12 gathered" said how MUCH was here and
		      nothing about what: three monsters and a map read at a glance where twelve of
		      something did not. Built in JS (`encounterTally`), zeroes already dropped, in the
		      fixed order the tally table declares -- so a glyph sits in the same place on every
		      card and a column of them is scanned down rather than read across.

		      THE SAME GROUPING THE BODY DRAWS ITS CARDS FROM (`groupEncounterEntries`), read here
		      for its counts and there for its rows. One array and not two passes, so the badge
		      row and the cards under it cannot disagree about what an arcanum is.

		      RENDERED ONLY WHEN THERE IS SOMETHING IN IT. An empty encounter drew a bordered "0"
		      that said only what its empty body already says, on the very card a GM is about to
		      drop the first thing onto.

		      THE COUNT COMES BEFORE ITS GLYPH, because that is the order it is read in ("three
		      monsters"). The glyph is `aria-hidden` and the tooltip says the kind in words, so
		      the badge is not a picture nobody can name. --}}
		{{#if groups.length}}
		<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-count">
			{{#each groups}}
			<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-tally stonetop-gm-encounter-tally--{{key}}"
			      data-tooltip="{{tip}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">{{count}}<i class="{{icon}}" aria-hidden="true"></i></span>
			{{/each}}
		</span>
		{{/if}}
		{{!-- THE CARET, at the right-hand end of the breakdown rather than at the head's left. It
		      is the badges a GM reads a shut column by, and the caret belongs to the same
		      question: this row says three monsters and a map, now show me them. Sat at the far
		      left it was the first thing on every row and about the last thing wanted there,
		      pushing the name in by 26px on a tab whose rows are mostly name.

		      OUTSIDE the badge row's `{{#if}}`, because an encounter with nothing gathered still
		      has a body to open: its notes box and the strip that things are dropped onto.

		      The glyph turns -90deg while shut (pointing right, the way every other disclosure in
		      the system turns) off `.is-collapsed` on the <li>, so moving the button changes
		      nothing about that. --}}
		<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-toggle" aria-expanded="{{#if open}}true{{else}}false{{/if}}"
		        aria-label="{{#if open}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.collapse"}}{{else}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.expand"}}{{/if}}">
			<i class="fas fa-caret-down"></i>
		</button>
		<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-tools">
			<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-remove"
			        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.removeEncounter"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
				<i class="fas fa-trash"></i>
			</button>
		</span>
	</div>

	<div class="stonetop-gm-encounter-body">
		{{!-- THE NOTE COMES FIRST, above the collected rows. What a GM writes here is what the
		      encounter IS ("they cross at dusk, the ford is watched"); the rows under it are the
		      documents it points at. Reading order follows that, and it puts the note at the same
		      place on every card rather than after a list that is two rows long on one and twelve
		      on the next. --}}
		<div class="stonetop-gm-encounter-notes-block">
			{{!-- THE NOTES READ AS A FIELD, not as a paragraph that happens to be there. Same box
			      the character and steading sheets give their own Notes tabs (`.editor-content` on
			      those, this on the read block): a translucent well, a hairline, and a pencil
			      parked in its top-right corner, all of which brighten together on hover. An
			      encounter holding no notes still reads as somewhere to write rather than as an
			      empty line, which is the whole reason those sheets box theirs too.

			      NOT A LIVE `<prose-mirror>`, which is what the character sheet actually puts in
			      that box. This sheet re-renders on every prep-page write ANYWHERE in the world, so
			      an editor open on this tab would be torn out from under a GM mid-sentence; the
			      pencil opens a Dialog, which is a window redraw cannot reach (see
			      bundle-notes-dialog.js). What is borrowed here is the box and its corner
			      control, not the editor inside it.

			      THE PENCIL IS THE BLOCK'S LAST CHILD so it can be parked in the corner over
			      whichever of the two blocks below was drawn. Revealed on hover, as core reveals
			      `<prose-mirror toggled>`'s own toggle. --}}
			{{#if notesHtml}}
			<div class="stonetop-gm-encounter-notes">{{{notesHtml}}}</div>
			{{else}}
			<p class="stonetop-gm-encounter-notes stonetop-gm-encounter-notes--empty">{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.notesEmpty"}}</p>
			{{/if}}
			<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-notes-edit"
			        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.notesEdit"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
				<i class="fas fa-pen"></i>
			</button>
		</div>

		{{!-- ONE CARD PER KIND, in the fixed order the tally table declares, and every kind the
		      encounter has none of simply absent. What was here was one flat column in which a
		      monster, a map, a page and a table were told apart only by a small glyph and a small
		      grey word at the far end of each row: everything looked alike and the eye had to read
		      every line to find the three monsters.

		      THE HEADING IS WHAT THE ROWS NO LONGER SAY. Each row used to carry its own glyph and
		      its own kind label, and under a card that has just said "Monsters and NPCs" both were
		      the same word twice. Saying it once, at the head of the group, is the whole of the
		      de-cluttering: a row is now a name, a note, and the cross that removes it.

		      `data-entry-kind` IS LOAD-BEARING, not a styling hook. The reorder gestures read it to
		      answer "could this row go where it was let go of": the body is grouped, so an index in
		      the stored array is no longer a place on screen, and a monster dropped among the maps
		      has nowhere there to land (see `_sameEntryKind` and `nudgeWithinGroup`). The drag
		      handle sends the kind of the card it came out of alongside core's own payload.

		      THE ROWS OF ONE CARD ARE STILL ONE <ul>, and the keyboard reorder reads exactly that
		      list to find the row above and below. Nest anything else that carries `data-entry-id`
		      inside it and alt+Arrow starts counting rows the GM cannot see. --}}
		{{#each groups}}
		<section class="stonetop-gm-entry-card" data-entry-kind="{{key}}">
			<div class="stonetop-gm-entry-card-head">
				<i class="{{icon}}" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				<span class="stonetop-gm-entry-card-title">{{title}}</span>
				{{!-- PUT THEM ALL ON THE MAP, on the heading of the card that holds them. In the
				      encounter's own head it was a dragon glyph two buttons from the trash, on a
				      row that says nothing about whether there is anything to place: an encounter
				      of nothing but journal pages wore it too, and pressing it there got a warning
				      instead of an act. Here it exists only where it can do something, beside the
				      count of exactly what it is about to place.

				      `canDeploy` is set in JS off the group's kind (`groupEncounterEntries`), not
				      tested here with a helper: what the button ACTS on is decided by
				      `_deployEncounter`, which filters on "Actor" itself, and two places deciding
				      "is this deployable" in two different ways is how they come to disagree.

				      IT SITS LEFT OF THE COUNT, which stays at the far end of every strip so a
				      column of cards can still be read down its right-hand edge. --}}
				{{#if canDeploy}}
				<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-deploy"
				        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.deploy"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
					{{!-- A MASK, not an <i>. The glyph is game-icons.net art (sbed's "pounce",
					      credited in assets/icons/ATTRIBUTION.md), and worn as a mask over
					      `currentColor` it takes this button's resting ink, its blue hover and both
					      themes off one file -- where an <img> would need a second copy and a
					      filter to fight. See the tab rail, which wears its whole set this way. --}}
					<span class="stonetop-gm-encounter-deploy-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
				</button>
				{{/if}}
				<span class="stonetop-gm-entry-card-count">{{count}}</span>
			</div>
			<ul class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entries">
				{{#each entries}}
				<li class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entry{{#if unresolved}} is-unresolved{{/if}}" data-entry-id="{{id}}">
					{{!-- A <button>, not an <a>: an href would make this natively draggable and
					      fight the drag it already emits, and it is a delegated handler either
					      way. See relationships-table.hbs, which says the same thing at
					      length. --}}
					<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entry-open"
					        draggable="{{#if unresolved}}false{{else}}true{{/if}}"
					        data-uuid="{{uuid}}" data-doc-type="{{type}}"
					        data-tooltip="{{#if unresolved}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.entryGoneTip"}}{{else}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.entryOpenTip"}}{{/if}}"
					        data-tooltip-direction="UP">{{name}}</button>
					<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entry-remove"
					        data-tooltip="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.removeEntry"}}" data-tooltip-direction="UP">
						<i class="fas fa-xmark"></i>
					</button>
					<input type="text" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-entry-note" value="{{note}}" maxlength="200"
					       placeholder="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.entryNotePlaceholder"}}"
					       aria-label="{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.entryNoteLabel"}}">
				</li>
				{{/each}}
			</ul>
		</section>
		{{/each}}

		{{!-- The visible target for "put it at the END". A drop on a row inserts BEFORE
		      that row, which is what keeps the whole router testable without measuring
		      pointer midpoints — and leaves no way to aim at the bottom of the list
		      except this strip. It doubles as the empty encounter's only content. --}}
		<p class="stonetop-gm-encounter-dropzone">{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.dropHere"}}</p>

		{{!-- THE ONE CONTROL THAT DIFFERS BETWEEN THE TWO LISTS, and it is one button rather than
		      two because it is one act read in either direction: the tick files an encounter down
		      into the Completed fold, and the arrow on a filed one lifts it back onto the live
		      list. The GLYPH turns with the tooltip, so a card says which way it is about to go
		      without being hovered — a tick still sitting on something already done reads as
		      "press me to finish it", and gets pressed.

		      IN THE BODY, AND SO ONLY ON AN OPEN CARD. It sat in the head beside Deploy and the
		      trash, where it was a bare tick two pixels from a delete button, on every row of a
		      shut column. Down here it is reached only by opening the card it is about to file,
		      which is the encounter a GM has just finished reading; the head is left to the three
		      things that are true of a shut row (its name, its note's first line, what is in it).

		      IT WEARS ITS WORDS. In the head there was no room for a label and the glyph had to
		      carry the whole meaning; at the foot of a body there is, and "Mark complete" needs no
		      hover to be understood. The long form of both directions stays as the tooltip.

		      LAST IN THE BODY, under the dropzone rather than over it: the strip above is where
		      the gathering ends, and this is what is done once there is nothing left to gather. --}}
		<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-encounter-used"
		        data-tooltip="{{#if used}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.markUnused"}}{{else}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.markUsed"}}{{/if}}"
		        data-tooltip-direction="UP">
			<i class="fas {{#if used}}fa-rotate-left{{else}}fa-check{{/if}}"></i> {{#if used}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.markUnusedShort"}}{{else}}{{localize "stonetop.gmToolkit.encounters.markUsedShort"}}{{/if}}
		</button>
	</div>
</li>
